#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229999} :activity/id #uuid "452d066f-9aab-563c-a587-2ed3d81298e4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048342 :topic/id #uuid "971f4f1b-99c0-555e-8afb-feeef118b307" :topic/math-academy-id 6704} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230244 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll derive a formula for variation of parameters for second-order linear ODEs.\n\nSuppose we're given the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + p(x)y' + q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nand let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\nRecall that the method of variation of parameters begins by assuming that a particular solution satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} &={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{'} &={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUsing these constraints, we will set up a system of two equations that we can solve for $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\n- To obtain the first equation, we differentiate the constraint for $y_p$ given above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =({u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2})^{'} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand since we assumed that $y_p' = u_1 y_1' + u_2 y_2',$ we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'} & ={u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'} \\\\ 0 & ={u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To obtain another equation, we substitute the constraints into the differential equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+p(x){y}^{'}+q(x)y & =f(x) \\\\ ({u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'})^{'}+p(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'})+q(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}) & =f(x) \\\\ ({u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{''}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{''})+p(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'})+q(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}) & =f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}({y}_{1}^{''}+p(x){y}_{1}^{'}+q(x){y}_{1})+{u}_{2}({y}_{2}^{''}+p(x){y}_{2}^{'}+q(x){y}_{2})+{u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1'' + p(x) y_1' + q(x)y_1=0$ and $y_2'' + p(x) y_2' + q(x) y_2=0$ since $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the corresponding homogeneous ODE. Thus, we get\n\n$$\nu_1' y_1' + u_2' y_2' = f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'} &=f\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the solution of the system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{'} & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}0 & {y}_{2} \\\\ f & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|}=-\\frac{{y}_{2}f}{{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}}=-\\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{'} & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & f\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|}=\\frac{{y}_{1}f}{{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}}=\\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nIntegrating, we reach the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ca38230-bd47-5c8e-a406-3348da7a971e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4152 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cbfa3100-96a1-5e81-8141-ae263cd302ed" :step/math-academy-id 591922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229140 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228954 :question/id #uuid "d7e995ec-7c1a-510d-be52-5ef3ce5f77aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-6618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049062}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365825}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots"} :step/id #uuid "8f214df2-6ee3-5385-9094-45bf89f34516" :step/math-academy-id 591923 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229141 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228953 :question/id #uuid "533cdb5e-1d90-5aae-961b-4fe7daa30b0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365838}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "15420a9f-3be5-50bf-a481-21a048c336e0" :step/math-academy-id 591926}] :activity/title "Solving Second-Order ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229263}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167642} :activity/id #uuid "7042cf87-d987-5e5d-80bf-d9af04e62830" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358 :topic/id #uuid "eea04b94-4b8a-5afe-a7ad-7279c5cf1588" :topic/math-academy-id 3282} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168071 :tutorial/content "A discrete random variable $X$ follows a **Poisson distribution** (pronounced \"pwaas-on\") if it has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe **rate parameter** $\\lambda$ is a positive real number, and\n\n$$\ne\\approx 2.71828\n$$\n\nis Euler's number.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a Poisson distribution, we write\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)\n$$\n\nThe Poisson distribution models the number of independent events $X$ that occur within a fixed interval of space or time. The parameter $\\lambda$ represents the mean number of events that occur in an interval (we'll justify this in a future lesson).\n\nFor example, suppose that an average of\n\n$$\n\\lambda={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nbuses stop at a particular bus shelter every $10$ minutes. The number of buses that stop at the shelter in a randomly selected $10$-minute period can be modeled by a Poisson random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Po}({\\color{blue}2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that\n\n$$\nX={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nbuses will stop at the shelter in the next $10$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= f(3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{3}e^{-2}}{3!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1804\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nWe will learn more about modeling situations using Poisson random variables shortly. But for now, let's concentrate on computing probabilities for Poisson random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fd6a3d4-a095-5f17-8527-5e6b17f4d813" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5030 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6e446eaa-af47-5f7a-a199-28e77e17fe31" :step/math-academy-id 31456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166654 :question/id #uuid "fba5b411-86fe-5d2f-85ee-87cfa1e96d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8465"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74510348-04f1-570b-b3f7-5fcb1ed1beef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670463}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "036aa9a7-bb92-5d00-ba65-ec3f305143b1" :step/math-academy-id 31518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166655 :question/id #uuid "62d1e798-9a57-5657-9a6d-8fb70bff0ccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8466"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "540361c1-5d4e-5b88-a295-f606f5518f38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167486}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "066e3107-bb37-5eb1-95af-02181840af06" :step/math-academy-id 31519 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166656 :question/id #uuid "c89b3081-38d7-5430-b912-82f84ef532ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8467"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21835800-1a05-56c1-a5ee-b8c2e1df2273" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167487}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Not Zero"} :step/id #uuid "0173dbc0-d2b4-5629-a48d-de97e9d25b57" :step/math-academy-id 31520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166657 :question/id #uuid "d1e693a2-073f-5f8c-98fd-3b89496521d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8468"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6219a2a-8c28-57c1-a4ad-0ede62529376" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167488}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "1e8e01c3-7ec6-5992-b850-be3724d44357" :step/math-academy-id 31521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168107 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function $f(x)$ with support $S$ to be a valid probability mass function for a discrete random variable $X$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nLet's check these two conditions for the Poisson distribution. First, let's write down the pmf of a Poisson random variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,3, \\ldots\n$$\n\nWe'll also make use of the following result:\n\n$$\ne^{\\lambda} = \\sum\\limits_{x = 0}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\lambda^x}{x!}\n$$\n\nYou'll learn where this result comes from when you study calculus. But for now, let's assume it's true.\n\nUsing this result, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nis true as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(x ∈ S)f(x) &= ∑_(x = 0)^(∞)f(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x = 0)^(∞)\\frac{λ^{x}e^{- λ}}{x!} \\\\\n&= e^{- λ}∑_(x = 0)^(∞)\\frac{λ^{x}}{x!} \\\\\n&= e^{- λ} \\cdot e^{λ} \\\\\n&= e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x$ because $\\lambda^x$, $e^{-\\lambda}$, and $x$! are all positive. Furthermore, because $f(x)$ is non-negative and the sum of all values is $1$, we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nfor all $x$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(x)$ is a valid probability mass function." :tutorial/id #uuid "32806a77-5615-54c5-adb0-f441385189a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5357 :tutorial/title "Justification That the Poisson Distribution Forms a Probability Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "78939e0d-595e-5a87-8656-d3e42cf8f110" :step/math-academy-id 33487}] :activity/title "The Poisson Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167044}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199326} :activity/id #uuid "e65e5013-8e69-5be7-a53f-8247c1f26c90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199263 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198240 :question/id #uuid "7d0da02a-d686-5bb1-ab44-00286ab01783" :question/math-academy-id "e-8690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446816}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "c833d29b-43fd-5a3a-83ae-7c9928c184c2" :step/math-academy-id 32292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199264}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199264 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198241 :question/id #uuid "9235c493-f092-5f14-b989-be3b4548a90d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8691"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446818}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"} :step/id #uuid "b82b3836-2ed6-522e-8a6b-e9301766e38d" :step/math-academy-id 32293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199484 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ | $(0, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7cc22f9f-d1be-570d-acd7-d3f2e0af7a51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7743 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bbfb776-26d8-5be7-abb8-cae6bab3294c" :step/math-academy-id 44831 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198047 :question/id #uuid "fb947756-3471-59c8-b3c4-9b21a1a91557" :question/math-academy-id "e-12457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496295}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "0e5ab67d-1254-5853-868a-8e8a117b0032" :step/math-academy-id 44833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199263}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198494}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496354} :activity/id #uuid "5b35fc89-ed07-5713-aff2-bd03957018fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0499cbca-daad-57e1-9934-25522e310898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278629} {:answer/id #uuid "08288e31-44a4-5cbd-b685-8435569179d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} {:answer/id #uuid "10043f84-5b2d-5986-8b89-d3b961ecb64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278735} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd71714-856d-5694-afb1-6a391106f770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280278} {:answer/id #uuid "fea569a7-1a86-5d69-9043-62f12b437f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42f751a-ec9a-5f92-8bd2-846f2ed3dd93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e45729b0-5cc3-5497-8a8a-a3af06f9f95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162780" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}\\le 25.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}<25$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=25.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=25\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-16y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,10y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -16y &=10\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ -2y(8+5\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 5,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(5,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 5,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-40$ | $25$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ and its minimum value is $-40.$"} :step/id #uuid "09e7c588-2af6-5084-a29b-554d52342a46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496344}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f121e3-ccfa-57b8-b6ef-a2e2cd0f3b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278810} {:answer/id #uuid "65b8a4fe-1d29-5ee2-ad9f-6c0583668e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} {:answer/id #uuid "76c3a733-f198-5a7c-a7fb-0c39f1adc2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279650} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf6ea47-eaeb-5270-89ec-aea8d1830cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279837} {:answer/id #uuid "944923f9-e97d-5493-8850-5c83f2a0ed3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} :answer-field/id #uuid "797a794d-3c44-5cfd-9959-01ae83cb5934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8c68071-089c-5bff-bc03-ba61a8eab03b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162788" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the minimum possible sum of three positive numbers whose product is $27.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the three numbers. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y+z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $1=0.$ Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz & =xz \\\\ yz-xz & =0 \\\\ (y-x)z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =27 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =27 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=3+3+3=9.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9,3,1) & =9+3+1 \\\\  & =13 \\\\  & >f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4eb40113-e4c3-5ada-8661-3205e80bd911" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2581670b-66e9-5d37-9926-806360eb8f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495594} {:answer/id #uuid "280f9803-91c8-596b-9d69-7a1755e015d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495597} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7fc2e4-a9ae-57b1-a57d-6f3e9b58edf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b25f91e-47c0-5de9-8785-cc22a420cdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495717} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a20fa1-2478-557f-919f-c382ef996ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495594} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd28c84e-3672-589b-b259-7ab643cdcbfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8d5ef6c-fa65-5bc9-a3b7-421525094db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-99271" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=y-3x$ given that  ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 250.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=250.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=250\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ y & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-3y=∓15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-15,5),\\,(15,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-15,5)$ | $(15,-5)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $50$ | $-50$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-15,5),$ and its maximum value is $50.$"} :step/id #uuid "698d6400-2972-5372-825e-a45e8f947672" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c47837c-91e7-575e-bb0e-eeba6e46e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361251} {:answer/id #uuid "70afc2df-aa10-56b3-bc23-c23074c89c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361336} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0908b9-98ee-5adc-877d-81b2bba50711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361454} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6f809c-461e-5ba5-8943-29549871ecb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361544} {:answer/id #uuid "b782daff-1cbf-5514-ba1c-f04c88f0cadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361544} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b59c33-2059-5031-b6c9-48e74e90a78e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7eb8a72b-0beb-5d49-b077-ac32cdeca74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99274" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=8x-3y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 35.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -3 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-4y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ 32{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-4y=∓4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4,1),\\,(4,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(4,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-35$ | $35$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4,-1),$ and its maximum value is $35.$"} :step/id #uuid "69fad04d-c841-5d4c-9ad6-3077f4690f00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1715e06f-e211-5b5b-b085-4dce3fe10cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278797} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae4dbb2-6216-51cc-9650-90dd9779ab6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279260} {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fb5f2f-a80f-5006-a9c4-be4750a711f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280294} {:answer/id #uuid "e19f9127-76af-507a-8b89-9b102194e39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a1c281-1dcb-592f-8bb1-786e3c4e7bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b349e078-f370-57a4-8287-923743beec39" :question/math-academy-id "q-162790" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "b9fe1e25-b17e-5b86-bc43-671e9951b1b9"}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495498}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512982} :activity/id #uuid "96f786d0-7a4e-5202-b49a-8bf736b34184" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364650} {:answer/id #uuid "f96459e6-4979-5896-b4e6-40cb50e3fdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "162\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364651} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe9f1a7-0904-5a92-b9f4-1e7ca2a2d4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364652} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20d337-eb61-592e-aeed-865846e45bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "369\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364653} {:answer/id #uuid "42cfec62-7180-5eb9-8dbb-e33d7fe7ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" :question/math-academy-id "q-99230" :question/problem "A rectangular metal tank without a lid should hold $256\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ of liquid. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the tank's minimum possible surface area." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y) &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d937a32-c217-5b66-9900-161e09432569" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c1fa27-09bb-576b-97dd-85dd401dc8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360976} {:answer/id #uuid "681a4a87-3b1d-58fd-b752-c86f6763e6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361305} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e663-64a3-56ec-a432-8a3dc5cde46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361480} {:answer/id #uuid "d174ba97-334a-5124-8075-96cc9ef5a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38c98d-8f6b-5cf8-b355-869da372bc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" :question/math-academy-id "q-99240" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "207020bf-20e1-5c08-9b5a-497678e3c3cc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512988}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1609399a-f596-5865-ab9c-6aadc92d23db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360926} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4ae6f5-4806-5c92-95fb-a3ec3dd390a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361102} {:answer/id #uuid "838bc3f9-5547-5aae-9ce5-c937af91a9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} {:answer/id #uuid "83aec126-3e7b-519c-90b5-432b36990afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361422} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4af1fe-8cbf-5c53-aeb4-2b02ab93e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" :question/math-academy-id "q-99163" :question/problem "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4b0537ae-3d5e-577c-abe8-31abae4b844f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e208ac1-85a1-5e96-b7fa-52323bca0603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361546} {:answer/id #uuid "a36edd82-90fe-5174-9433-626f33ab66c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361567} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe3af3e-8a8f-5c90-9dc6-fba808b09e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361694} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5aea1-7458-5a36-bfcd-ed941a842039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361700} {:answer/id #uuid "ca55e0f9-7162-5f22-b015-8e0165d10253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361694} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1f27a1-0eda-517d-a4eb-016e3810f8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37949110-2c01-56b7-86c7-c788694289e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-99255" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+10{y}^{2}$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4$ is $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$"} :step/id #uuid "5a12f7cf-20a1-5a1b-867f-1e2e73b2103a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346a190f-bdeb-55f3-9684-8f7a67706bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446752} {:answer/id #uuid "69bcd4e4-7735-5f84-ad2b-2296929423e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446764} {:answer/id #uuid "75897010-0df0-514d-90ad-2e160e86599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446765} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3016dc-494e-58a7-af10-066972c0ac56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446780} {:answer/id #uuid "df3ed43d-6192-5e7c-985a-432c70a7ae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446765} :answer-field/id #uuid "6424ee71-260c-5a9c-9520-211ab5878bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c584a36-c1bc-53fc-9ad2-f5f8790f1c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-99270" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3y$ given that ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 14.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=14.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=14\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -3 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Substituting $x=-2y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=14,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-2y=∓2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-7\\sqrt{2}$ | $7\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-7\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "619b8315-b541-50e9-a444-8d549e33e3d2"}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512089}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524088} :activity/id #uuid "4ec68829-0394-5492-8dd6-3692fcfc1d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294a6497-3763-569f-bdf9-42356cd00564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361019} {:answer/id #uuid "52b8263c-0e8f-52be-ba17-ae87e84b31f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361202} {:answer/id #uuid "52e615b0-5300-5122-b210-2d048d12e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361204} {:answer/id #uuid "96f252cb-20d0-588c-88d0-dd5df5d6f960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361510} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3d1ba2-d9d5-5815-a75e-960a8bfa5fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361202} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a25a4a-3331-5eca-802d-4fffe8f3048b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c427251e-04fb-5644-a763-9c09169a9227" :question/math-academy-id "q-98104" :question/problem "A rectangular box without a lid has a surface area of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum possible volume of the box." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y$, and $z$ be the box's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n2xz+2yz+xy=48.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=2xz+2yz+xy.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2z+y,2z+x,2x+2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}yz &=\\lambda (2z+y) \\\\ xz &=\\lambda (2z+x) \\\\ xy &=\\lambda (2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xyz &=\\lambda x(2z+y) \\\\ xyz &=\\lambda y(2z+x) \\\\ xyz &=\\lambda z(2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $xyz=0,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2z+y) & =y(2z+x) \\\\ 2xz+xy & =2yz+xy \\\\ xz & =yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ne 0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz+2yz+xy & =48 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ 12{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(4,4,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(4,4,2)=4\\cdot 4\\cdot 2=32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,5.5) & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5.5 \\\\  & =22 \\\\  & <f(4,4,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b2d2a8b-c653-55db-b415-039fee6fd0b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940363d2-e432-556a-b0c0-2c42895c1117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198003} {:answer/id #uuid "20e54280-ff88-5af2-8610-6ef05e375392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198004} {:answer/id #uuid "2740310c-d926-5b3d-916c-6755f45ae1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198005} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdb73f9-d522-5716-a40d-01eb373017ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198006} {:answer/id #uuid "70c9a7e7-b1ad-5149-9703-aa84c63a4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198006} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd5a22f3-e62c-5edf-b899-6619cb4a4071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73adc7f1-2e5c-592b-b8c9-bd080977f54f" :question/math-academy-id "q-99256" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<16$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-6y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=8\\lambda x \\\\ -6y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(3+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 4).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 4),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 4)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-48$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm 4),$ and its minimum value is $-48.$"} :step/id #uuid "214df3d6-efb3-56b8-a79a-9d19a508ecbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26df7659-281a-55ad-9717-8a068ed8d410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446748} {:answer/id #uuid "29ba9e62-696e-5df4-a785-8720b21f1d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446750} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3270e4-ba18-50fc-871d-abe0c18290ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446786} {:answer/id #uuid "e475e7cd-dfed-5fbd-ae10-5b0c7895d481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab2ac1e-1760-5933-be14-b547164eb087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162781" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-2\\,⇒\\,y=-4.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(-4)^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$"} :step/id #uuid "8ff88094-3c99-5b7f-a06a-3099afb04e32"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17db0ffb-b770-5981-ad25-771eba941c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197968} {:answer/id #uuid "3b386300-4c24-597f-9204-ff433d9b9e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197969} {:answer/id #uuid "7b766f86-afe6-5a6b-af92-1c826d2db774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446769} {:answer/id #uuid "9cff40e6-e6af-52b0-9794-92138bab247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446776} {:answer/id #uuid "ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779} :answer-field/id #uuid "943b68fc-e739-5e05-8b0d-6d7886409b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76ff6072-ae0f-589d-9844-098ccc148f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-98102" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b28a72ae-5376-5f04-8f27-20d4b6edd06e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08059ff9-d83f-540a-a482-2a8f806a18c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399345} {:answer/id #uuid "40d83586-f330-5ddd-a884-c230dd198024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399346} {:answer/id #uuid "591f3fa0-6797-5340-8094-49d07c9c3d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399347} {:answer/id #uuid "44d35100-e5d6-5e35-ac5b-f307d1753f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399348} {:answer/id #uuid "1396c469-16f9-566a-aa93-62900910c424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399347} :answer-field/id #uuid "25347463-2d58-522d-8979-b8544d1335f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e5f2d5d-b1b9-503b-9dbd-36de49acff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-99204" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $108.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}yz &=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz &=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xyz &=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz &=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz &=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c76a4af5-1fa8-565f-8538-01592aa5b8dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524046}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523240}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545888} :activity/id #uuid "0ac63a17-8eba-58fd-81f6-c5dfcfca821d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "839287e3-b143-5415-99de-08e22d1fe7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197973} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac8fd50-dcb6-5b86-b402-c970434163b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197974} {:answer/id #uuid "fca362ab-06c7-5e0f-a0ea-d44fa5a42449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197975} {:answer/id #uuid "401f21c4-4ada-5f5e-b68f-b4edd51e55ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197976} {:answer/id #uuid "4d850692-8440-57f8-a931-14cb51d6f903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197973} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b076631-de18-587b-978a-08fc530f03da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ab0253d0-1db9-532d-bb4a-604c88a16da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98103" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 18.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(2,2).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=18.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=18\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y-4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(3,3),\\,(-3,-3),\\,(2,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(3,3)$ | $(-3,-3)$ | $(2,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $42$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-3,-3),$ and its maximum value is $42.$"} :step/id #uuid "0ef9d217-5318-56ef-a70a-b3b1bdd62fac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bacdbdc-a887-5be8-858f-c82cd078c3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446746} {:answer/id #uuid "42ad9543-9de3-59fd-b83c-d669cb4d5f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446755} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8fe052-2121-5c2b-b562-5b0acfc936f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446759} {:answer/id #uuid "8edd449c-a525-5941-972e-625e39f9c583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446773} {:answer/id #uuid "980a9376-7517-58ca-ab4f-a3dde8b299a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446775} :answer-field/id #uuid "9450a1b5-7499-5e9b-839d-b2e01f9b40ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c5088ac-9a02-5898-afc8-f9931b4dcdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162800" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+y=2.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives us the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -4y &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{4},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =2\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{17}{8}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a66e406f-6c08-5429-b179-f50c725ecf90"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364650} {:answer/id #uuid "f96459e6-4979-5896-b4e6-40cb50e3fdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "162\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364651} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe9f1a7-0904-5a92-b9f4-1e7ca2a2d4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364652} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20d337-eb61-592e-aeed-865846e45bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "369\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364653} {:answer/id #uuid "42cfec62-7180-5eb9-8dbb-e33d7fe7ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" :question/math-academy-id "q-99230" :question/problem "A rectangular metal tank without a lid should hold $256\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ of liquid. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the tank's minimum possible surface area." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y) &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ad8a33d6-0b0a-5996-8da1-7927904bffbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1609399a-f596-5865-ab9c-6aadc92d23db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360926} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4ae6f5-4806-5c92-95fb-a3ec3dd390a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361102} {:answer/id #uuid "838bc3f9-5547-5aae-9ce5-c937af91a9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} {:answer/id #uuid "83aec126-3e7b-519c-90b5-432b36990afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361422} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4af1fe-8cbf-5c53-aeb4-2b02ab93e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" :question/math-academy-id "q-99163" :question/problem "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eef0b459-bbf6-5186-8922-6b8989aa4de0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0499cbca-daad-57e1-9934-25522e310898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278629} {:answer/id #uuid "08288e31-44a4-5cbd-b685-8435569179d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} {:answer/id #uuid "10043f84-5b2d-5986-8b89-d3b961ecb64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278735} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd71714-856d-5694-afb1-6a391106f770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280278} {:answer/id #uuid "fea569a7-1a86-5d69-9043-62f12b437f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42f751a-ec9a-5f92-8bd2-846f2ed3dd93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e45729b0-5cc3-5497-8a8a-a3af06f9f95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162780" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}\\le 25.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}<25$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=25.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=25\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-16y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,10y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -16y &=10\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ -2y(8+5\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 5,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(5,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 5,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-40$ | $25$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ and its minimum value is $-40.$"} :step/id #uuid "f98db47a-48c0-5d3a-ba0a-051c09b08ff1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545962}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545183}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580344} :activity/id #uuid "30ace273-630b-5110-b8d8-ef18d6018f35" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2581670b-66e9-5d37-9926-806360eb8f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495594} {:answer/id #uuid "280f9803-91c8-596b-9d69-7a1755e015d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495597} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7fc2e4-a9ae-57b1-a57d-6f3e9b58edf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b25f91e-47c0-5de9-8785-cc22a420cdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495717} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a20fa1-2478-557f-919f-c382ef996ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495594} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd28c84e-3672-589b-b259-7ab643cdcbfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8d5ef6c-fa65-5bc9-a3b7-421525094db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-99271" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=y-3x$ given that  ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 250.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=250.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=250\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ y & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-3y=∓15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-15,5),\\,(15,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-15,5)$ | $(15,-5)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $50$ | $-50$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-15,5),$ and its maximum value is $50.$"} :step/id #uuid "43f9abfe-835b-57a3-a40a-e05dda3e5a84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17db0ffb-b770-5981-ad25-771eba941c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197968} {:answer/id #uuid "3b386300-4c24-597f-9204-ff433d9b9e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197969} {:answer/id #uuid "7b766f86-afe6-5a6b-af92-1c826d2db774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446769} {:answer/id #uuid "9cff40e6-e6af-52b0-9794-92138bab247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446776} {:answer/id #uuid "ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779} :answer-field/id #uuid "943b68fc-e739-5e05-8b0d-6d7886409b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76ff6072-ae0f-589d-9844-098ccc148f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-98102" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "6aabbbcb-7bab-5819-997c-0fe293e0f792" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c1fa27-09bb-576b-97dd-85dd401dc8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360976} {:answer/id #uuid "681a4a87-3b1d-58fd-b752-c86f6763e6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361305} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e663-64a3-56ec-a432-8a3dc5cde46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361480} {:answer/id #uuid "d174ba97-334a-5124-8075-96cc9ef5a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38c98d-8f6b-5cf8-b355-869da372bc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" :question/math-academy-id "q-99240" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "8075bb6c-56c7-5a23-bc58-470ac22fc76e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516e7492-e083-54f4-b969-813cd1e7e379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446758} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b0972e-d4be-5359-ad60-bb6bb0f92728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446784} {:answer/id #uuid "c11354ec-35c7-5aa0-87c4-54a1c16aac24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446787} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a79e2b-437a-5e9b-ac4c-f92bd6d086fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446788} {:answer/id #uuid "d21bbc09-d6e4-59ee-99da-258932620c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446791} :answer-field/id #uuid "414dce70-34d5-521d-80c0-bd1e958ec286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74a32659-ab2b-5195-9c2f-351117b323e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162778" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{3}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b688d1a0-ca62-52cb-9676-d18b65239a76"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346a190f-bdeb-55f3-9684-8f7a67706bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446752} {:answer/id #uuid "69bcd4e4-7735-5f84-ad2b-2296929423e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446764} {:answer/id #uuid "75897010-0df0-514d-90ad-2e160e86599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446765} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3016dc-494e-58a7-af10-066972c0ac56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446780} {:answer/id #uuid "df3ed43d-6192-5e7c-985a-432c70a7ae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446765} :answer-field/id #uuid "6424ee71-260c-5a9c-9520-211ab5878bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c584a36-c1bc-53fc-9ad2-f5f8790f1c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-99270" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3y$ given that ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 14.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=14.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=14\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -3 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Substituting $x=-2y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=14,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-2y=∓2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-7\\sqrt{2}$ | $7\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-7\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "ef381327-08ac-5c1a-a57e-496b478d32ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580332}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579669}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060674} :activity/id #uuid "780b6d06-4e3c-5fb9-a687-1ad2687e0771" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050953 :topic/id #uuid "7525f3f4-fbe5-5024-8fab-55ad3066edd8" :topic/math-academy-id 266} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059519 :question/id #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" :question/math-academy-id "e-4233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670461}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "a0e30d07-9c41-5c2c-839c-9e49ca64764c" :step/math-academy-id 15458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059520 :question/id #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670456}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "233132be-0fc4-52d1-9152-818b8c45b168" :step/math-academy-id 15459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059521 :question/id #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-4235"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c24d70-3bde-5277-bc92-ae081065deee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670459}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial and a Monomial in Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "13e292aa-c881-5526-9904-6d174bbb856b" :step/math-academy-id 15460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :question/id #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4236"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387a794f-17ea-5ea9-86a1-656a495f62cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670462}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations with Binomials in Both Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "634f203a-900b-5f9f-977b-e1bb2ab2c5ab" :step/math-academy-id 15461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060937 :tutorial/content "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x \\\\ 10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "33bd96be-8ef8-525c-be19-8590b4893b52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3123 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b043ea3a-fd3a-5de4-b7ac-21a29627ca53" :step/math-academy-id 17016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060580}}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059941}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540116} :activity/id #uuid "1c136286-07e7-54b0-87c2-d0c00e24e994" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050953 :topic/id #uuid "7525f3f4-fbe5-5024-8fab-55ad3066edd8" :topic/math-academy-id 266} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe93af-c815-5f2c-ac75-9a584d561ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058879} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d50060-ed5a-55ba-98a5-e136d622f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7edad-f24a-5a39-b3a9-bdf0e222d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058881} {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} {:answer/id #uuid "4335e387-f8cb-50d1-a3a5-f1a739c9876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} :answer-field/id #uuid "084ec80c-c0ed-58a4-b621-614f03d00659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09320d7a-d867-531b-9740-66504912149d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67332" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{4}\\right) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04bd31c0-ddc2-5b9c-ad07-d73251e35927" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d56b3b6-d2ee-5a6a-ac79-f166c602aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539536} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc6a18-26ef-5ee7-a216-046a93109cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539634} {:answer/id #uuid "684d8919-d630-53ec-be88-da83985b9cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539650} {:answer/id #uuid "adf72623-7732-5460-b0b6-5cc1011adb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539775} {:answer/id #uuid "f71975b1-e674-5e94-a227-f47c16e02797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894} :answer-field/id #uuid "746714c2-cc0f-5a43-9606-b934d8793273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dba16e2f-f755-5c6f-b25b-801d348b9282" :question/math-academy-id "q-67435" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{4}{x-2}=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{x-1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $4.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{4x-4}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-2}{-4}\\right) & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{4x-4}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ x+4x & =4+2 \\\\ 5x & =6 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cdot (5x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cdot (6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cdot (6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "128aaed4-1578-549e-bc3a-0ca44706efdb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e054d1-ef35-5151-b5a6-95077b0323c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539572} {:answer/id #uuid "5deed0da-0520-5246-8e6c-365d802468b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539637} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5af59e-eefa-5320-9d2c-7a4fb55193a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539660} {:answer/id #uuid "a816dc9c-912f-5b96-8ae4-1b55000c74ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539765} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e754c7-eafd-5c39-98a7-4f2732e90d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539660} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7cb7b4d-8e3a-52e0-b39c-57977a95c75f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9929bc33-2631-5ab2-871b-80ee305fc700" :question/math-academy-id "q-67413" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+5=3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =3 \\\\ x+5-5 & =3-5 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5a1efe66-123d-5aba-9cc1-514789a8cab9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540123}}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539465}]}
